    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/05/brooks-hyperion-max-3-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Hyperion Max 3 is a true max-cushioned setup (45 mm heel / 37 mm forefoot) with high impact protection and a slightly soft but still firm, controlled underfoot feel.",
        "Responsiveness: The PEBA-based DNA Gold foam and stiff SpeedVault plate deliver strong bounce/energy return and a quick, snappy response when pace picks up.",
        "Stability: Torsional/forefoot rigidity is high (very little flexibility), and the rigid heel counter plus secure, reinforced upper provide strong heel hold and lateral support.",
        "Durability: The review’s stated durability expectation is average, with no major outsole-wear red flags noted and upper materials appearing sturdy but not highlighted as exceptionally long-lasting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/06/brooks-hyperion-max-3-multi-tester.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Hyperion Max 3 uses a dual supercritical midsole (PEBA DNA Gold on top, firmer Flash v2 below), jumps to a 46/40 mm stack, and is described as softer, more comfortable, and more protective than v2.",
        "Responsiveness: The nylon plate plus RapidRoll rocker provide moderate propulsion and good pace range, but multiple testers found v3 less snappy and agile than v2, with a stiffer overall ride.",
        "Stability: Testers call it one of the most stable super-max trainers, citing the rigid heel counter, secure heel/midfoot lockdown, and supportive platform for strong rearfoot/lateral control.",
        "Durability: Direct v3 durability evidence is limited in the provided text; no specific outsole-wear complaints are reported, and only prior v2 longevity (~250 miles for one tester) is explicitly noted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/brooks-hyperion-max-3-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Hyperion Max 3 is now true max-stack (46 mm heel/40 mm forefoot), giving strong impact protection for long runs, but the ride is described as more firm-than-soft underfoot.",
        "Responsiveness: The DNA Gold PEBA top layer provides noticeable bounce, and the RapidRoll rocker gives a smooth landing-to-toe-off transition; it is more of a rolling cruiser than a snappy tempo shoe.",
        "Stability: The review repeatedly notes some instability (\"somewhat unstable,\" \"squirrelly\" at times), especially on uneven terrain/walking, but fit lockdown is solid with no heel slip and it feels fairly stable once moving.",
        "Durability: It uses RoadTack outsole rubber and handled a rough 10-mile gravel run without major problems, but the text does not provide strong long-term outsole wear or material longevity data."
      ]
    }
